/** @mainpage Qore geos Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section geosintro Introduction

    The geos module provides <a href="https://libgeos.org">GEOS (Geometry Engine - Open Source)</a> bindings for
    %Qore, enabling fast GIS data processing with spatial predicates, topology operations, spatial indexing, and
    WKT/WKB serialization.

    This module is released under the MIT license (see COPYING.MIT in the source distribution for more information).

    The module is tagged as such in the module's header (meaning it can be loaded unconditionally regardless of how
    the %Qore library was initialized).

    Like all Qore components, the geos module is thread-safe. Each thread lazily initializes its own GEOS context
    handle using the reentrant GEOS C API (\c _r variants).

    The underlying geometry functionality is provided by <a href="https://libgeos.org">GEOS (libgeos)</a>.

    The module has two layers:
    - <b>C++ binary module</b> (\c geos): GEOSGeometry, GEOSPreparedGeometry, GEOSSTRtree, and
      WKT/WKB reader/writer classes for in-memory geometry operations
    - <b>@ref geosdataproviderintro "GEOSDataProvider"</b>: data provider integration for reading and
      writing WKT/WKB geometry files in data pipelines (load with \c %%requires \c GEOSDataProvider)

    @section geosclasses Classes

    The following classes are provided:
    - @ref Qore::GEOS::GEOSGeometry "GEOSGeometry" - Core geometry wrapper with spatial predicates, topology
      operations, distance calculations, linear referencing, coordinate access, and WKT/WKB serialization
    - @ref Qore::GEOS::GEOSPreparedGeometry "GEOSPreparedGeometry" - Pre-indexed geometry for fast repeated
      spatial queries
    - @ref Qore::GEOS::GEOSSTRtree "GEOSSTRtree" - STR-packed R-tree spatial index for envelope intersection
      queries and nearest-neighbor searches
    - @ref Qore::GEOS::GEOSWKTReader "GEOSWKTReader" - WKT parsing
    - @ref Qore::GEOS::GEOSWKTWriter "GEOSWKTWriter" - WKT serialization with formatting options
    - @ref Qore::GEOS::GEOSWKBReader "GEOSWKBReader" - WKB parsing (binary and hex)
    - @ref Qore::GEOS::GEOSWKBWriter "GEOSWKBWriter" - WKB serialization (binary and hex)

    @section geosfunctions Standalone Functions

    - @ref Qore::GEOS::geos_version() "geos_version()" - Returns the GEOS version string
    - @ref Qore::GEOS::get_geos_info() "get_geos_info()" - Returns detailed GEOS version information
    - @ref Qore::GEOS::parse_wkt() "parse_wkt()" - Convenience WKT parsing
    - @ref Qore::GEOS::make_wkt() "make_wkt()" - Convenience WKT serialization
    - @ref Qore::GEOS::parse_wkb() "parse_wkb()" - Convenience WKB parsing
    - @ref Qore::GEOS::make_wkb() "make_wkb()" - Convenience WKB serialization

    @section geostypes Type Mappings

    | GEOS Type | Qore Type |
    |-----------|-----------|
    | Coordinate | \c hash<GEOSCoordinate> |
    | Geometry | @ref Qore::GEOS::GEOSGeometry "GEOSGeometry" |
    | WKT string | \c string |
    | WKB data | \c binary |

    @section geosexamples Examples

    @par Create a geometry from WKT and perform operations:
    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires geos

GEOSGeometry poly = new GEOSGeometry("POLYGON ((0 0, 10 0, 10 10, 0 10, 0 0))");
GEOSGeometry point = new GEOSGeometry("POINT (5 5)");

printf("contains: %y\n", poly.contains(point));  # True
printf("area: %y\n", poly.area());                # 100.0
printf("centroid: %y\n", make_wkt(poly.centroid())); # "POINT (5 5)"
    @endcode

    @par Use prepared geometry for fast repeated queries:
    @code{.py}
GEOSGeometry poly = new GEOSGeometry("POLYGON ((0 0, 10 0, 10 10, 0 10, 0 0))");
GEOSPreparedGeometry pg = poly.prepare();
for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) {
    GEOSGeometry pt = parse_wkt(sprintf("POINT (%d %d)", i % 10, i / 100));
    if (pg.contains(pt)) {
        # handle contained point
    }
}
    @endcode

    @par Use STRtree for spatial indexing:
    @code{.py}
GEOSSTRtree tree(10);
tree.insert(parse_wkt("POINT (1 1)"), "A");
tree.insert(parse_wkt("POINT (2 2)"), "B");
tree.insert(parse_wkt("POINT (100 100)"), "C");

list<auto> results = tree.query(parse_wkt("POLYGON ((0 0, 5 0, 5 5, 0 5, 0 0))"));
# results contains "A" and "B"
    @endcode

    @par Read geometry from a WKT file using the GEOSDataProvider module:
    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires GEOSDataProvider

# Read WKT file (one geometry per line)
GEOSWktReadDataProvider provider({"path": "shapes.wkt"});
map printf("%s: %s\n", $1.type, $1.geometry), provider.searchRecords();

# Write geometries to a WKB hex file
GEOSWkbWriteDataProvider writer({"path": "output.wkb"});
writer.createRecord({"geometry": "POLYGON ((0 0, 10 0, 10 10, 0 10, 0 0))"});
    @endcode

    See the @ref geosdataproviderintro "GEOSDataProvider module documentation" for more examples.

    @section geosbuilding Building

    The geos module requires the GEOS library with C API headers to build.

    @subsection geosbuildreqs Build Requirements

    | Distribution | Packages |
    |-------------|----------|
    | Ubuntu / Debian | \c libgeos-dev |
    | Fedora / RHEL | \c geos-devel |
    | Alpine | \c geos-dev |

    @subsection geosbuildsteps Build Steps

    @code{.sh}
    mkdir build
    cd build
    cmake ..
    make
    make install
    make test
    @endcode

    If Qore or GEOS cannot be found automatically:
    @code{.sh}
    cmake .. -DQORE_DIR=/path/to/qore -DGEOS_DIR=/path/to/geos
    @endcode

